s=para(s,'4.2 The exact integral',r'''4.2 The exact integral upper basis and the form identification. On the QG standard frame the ambient form is orthonormal. For an addition $\mu=\nu+\gamma$ of residue $i$, the node coefficient of $F_i$ is $q^{N_i^r}$, while that of $q^{-1}K_iE_i$ on the reverse edge is $q^{N_i(\nu)-1-N_i^l}$. The splitting identity $N_i^r+N_i^l=N_i(\nu)-1$ makes them equal ([[def-the-node-order-and-n-i-exponents-for-fock-space]], [[thm-the-fock-space-node-formulas-satisfy-the-quantum-affine-relations]]). The diagonal toral operators are self-adjoint, and symmetry supplies the reverse $E_i$ identity; thus the ambient form is contravariant for $\sigma(F_i)=q^{-1}K_iE_i$ and $\sigma(E_i)=qF_iK_i^{-1}$. Its restriction to the vacuum basic module is normalized by $B(\varnothing,\varnothing)=1$, and uniqueness of the normalized highest-weight contravariant form identifies it with the QG form ([[def-contravariant-form-on-an-integrable-quantum-highest-weight-module]]). The linear relabelling $J$ permutes the orthonormal standard frame and transports the action, so the same identification holds on $M_{\mathrm{sym}}$. Consequently [F3] makes the vectors of step 2.2 the transported upper global basis, free over $A$ in the restricted integral dual lattice and perfectly paired with the lower $A$-span. Their standard-frame coefficients can be rational as in step 3.1; this does not contradict integrality in the upper basis and implies no lower-$A$ selfduality or $A$-unit Gram matrix. [given, F3, step 2.2, step 3.1]''')
