s=para(s,'[F2] The restricted',r'''[F2] The restricted lower vectors expand as $G(\lambda)=\sum_\mu d_{(\lambda,\mu)}(q)M_\mu$, with diagonal coefficient $1$, off-diagonal coefficients in $q\mathbb Z[q]$, and support only when $\mu$ strictly dominates $\lambda$ off the diagonal ([[def-level-one-global-basis-transition-polynomials-in-the-symmetric-group-convention]], [[thm-the-basic-representation-global-basis-is-unitriangular-in-the-partition-basis]]).''')
s=para(s,'[F3] The lower',r'''[F3] On the basic subspace, the ambient orthonormal form restricts to the normalized highest-weight contravariant form, and its inverse-Gram dual family $G^*(\lambda)$ is therefore exactly the transported upper global basis. It is an $A$-basis of the restricted integral weight dual, with $B(G(\lambda),G^*(\lambda'))=\delta_{\lambda\lambda'}$. This form identification and ambient-coordinate construction are proved in [[def-level-one-global-basis-transition-polynomials-in-the-symmetric-group-convention]], step 4.2; the abstract upper duality and charts are [[def-upper-global-basis-by-the-contravariant-form]] and [[thm-upper-and-lower-global-bases-are-dual-and-crystal-compatible]].''')
s=para(s,'1.1 **The restricted',r'''1.1 **The restricted coefficient matrix is dominance-unitriangular.** Fix a content, and take the square minor $D_{\mathrm{res}}$ with rows and columns the restricted labels and row-$\mu$, column-$\lambda$ entry $d_{(\lambda,\mu)}$. By [F2], the diagonal is $1$ and the only off-diagonal support has $\mu$ strictly dominating $\lambda$. Choose a finite linear order refining this dominance direction; then $D_{\mathrm{res}}=I+N$ is triangular with nilpotent $N$ and coefficients in $\mathbb Z[q]$, and $(I+N)^{-1}=\sum_{k\ge0}(-N)^k$ is a finite polynomial matrix. In particular $\det D_{\mathrm{res}}=1$ and the minor is invertible over $A$. [F2, F4]''')
s=para(s,'2.1 **The restricted form',r'''2.1 **The restricted form is nondegenerate.** Let $D$ be the rectangular matrix with all standard rows and restricted lower columns on the fixed content. The Gram matrix is $H=D^{\mathsf t}D$ by [F1]. By [F2] its columns at $q=0$ are distinct coordinate vectors, so $H(0)=I$ and $\det H(0)=1$. Thus $H$ is invertible over $\mathbb Q(q)$; the form on the basic subspace is nondegenerate and identifies its field dual with that subspace. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]''')
s=s.replace('V^\\perp=C\\otimes\\mathbb Q(q)', 'V^\\perp').replace('with values in the lower $A$-lattice whenever $x$ lies in it', 'with values in $A$ whenever $x$ lies in the lower $A$-lattice')
s=para(s,'6.1 **The canonical recursion',r'''6.1 **The canonical recursion, finiteness and Choice.** All sums at fixed content are finite. The form identification of [F3] gives $B(F_ix,y)=B(x,q^{-1}K_iE_iy)$, and both $V$ and $V^\perp$ are stable under the quantum action: for $x\in V^\perp$ and $y\in V$, contravariance gives $B(ux,y)=B(x,\sigma(u)y)=0$. Hence the orthogonal projection $\pi$ commutes with the action. In the symmetric linear-$J$ model, conjugating the QG diagram changes lower-content nodes to above nodes and reflects colors; the transported removal formula is therefore $E_iM_\mu=\sum_{A\ \mathrm{removable}}q^{-d^A(\mu)}M_{\mu\setminus A}$, where $d^A$ is the addable-minus-removable count strictly above $A$ in the smaller diagram. Applying $\pi$ gives the same recursion for $\operatorname{Std}_\mu$, as used by the later Specht comparison. No new choice is made; [C1] is inherited. [F1, F3, C1, step 3.1] ∎''')
