consumer='thm-graded-specht-heads-classify-the-graded-simple-modules';live=Path('items',consumer+'.md');(p/'before'/(consumer+'.md')).write_bytes(live.read_bytes());s=live.read_text()
s=para(s,'1.2 **The distinguished',r'''1.2 **The distinguished shapes are the restricted ones (2).** By [F3] every standard coordinate functional has an upper expansion supported on restricted $\nu\unlhd\mu$ for its standard shape $\mu$. The restricted coefficient minor is unitriangular; its finite triangular inverse expresses each restricted upper vector as a combination of restricted standard functionals with labels no larger than its own. Consequently, for an unrestricted $\mu$, the class $[S(\mu)]$ is an $A$-combination of restricted Specht classes with labels strictly dominated by $\mu$. By the cellular purity of [F1], the nonzero-form head at label $\mu$, if it existed, could not occur in any of those smaller cell modules, whereas it occurs once in its own cell class; coefficient comparison in the simple-class basis excludes that head. Thus all distinguished cell labels are restricted. Their number is the rank of the simple-class Grothendieck basis, which equals the number of restricted labels by the restricted Specht basis in [F3], so every restricted label is distinguished. The diagonal-one and strictly smaller-factor clauses for these nonzero heads now follow from [F1], yielding (2). [F1, F3]''')
